# White House alert ledger is append-only and id-deduped on read (build_whitehouse
# ._load_ledger keeps the last row per id), so the correct conflict resolution is a
# UNION of both sides — never "keep only mine". The hourly sentinel is the sole writer,
# but this guarantees no alert row can be silently dropped even on an unexpected
# concurrent push.
data/whitehouse/alerts.jsonl merge=union

# The Universal Scoreboard claim + grade ledgers (engine/qledger.py) are
# append-only JSONL, deduped on read by claim_id (register()/register_batch()
# `dedupe=True` — a duplicated line is inert). Multiple lanes append here on
# the same night: asia-close.yml + daily.yml both run scripts/backfill_qledger_
# {us,cn,intel_hub}.py, whitehouse-sentinel.yml appends alert claims hourly,
# and Eval OS P3 (engine/qledger_desk_adapter.py) adds three more nightly
# writers (stock_desk, thematic_desk, demand_ledger). None of these lanes'
# pushes carried merge=union before P3 (B4 in the P3 brief) — a real rebase
# conflict in the shared push-retry loop (scripts/ci/push_retry.sh's
# `git pull --rebase --autostash -X theirs`) resolves in favour of the LOCAL
# replayed commit and silently drops the conflicting hunk from whatever was
# already pushed upstream, i.e. a genuine, already-registered forward claim
# can vanish with no error anywhere. A dropped row here is not an inert
# duplicate like the id-deduped stores above; it is DATA LOSS on the one
# record this program's forward-evidence claim depends on never losing.
data/qledger/claims.jsonl merge=union
data/qledger/grades.jsonl merge=union

# Causal hypothesis factory ledgers are append-only JSONL files.  The trial
# ledger (trial_ledger.jsonl) is written by BOTH the cortex nightly job and the
# new CHF causal job on the same night; a rebase conflict in the push-retry loop
# would silently drop causal rows and under-count CHF-R3 width.  All five causal
# JSONL files carry union merge to prevent any row loss on concurrent pushes.
data/trial_ledger.jsonl merge=union
data/neuralweb/causal_edges.jsonl merge=union
data/neuralweb/causal_nulls.jsonl merge=union
data/neuralweb/causal_surprise_queue.jsonl merge=union
data/neuralweb/causal_mechanisms.jsonl merge=union

# Marketing outbox ledgers are append-only and folded id-first on read
# (engine/marketing/outbox.fold_state keeps the FIRST row per item id and skips
# any ledger row whose transition is illegal from the folded state), so a
# duplicated row is inert and a DROPPED row loses a post's status forever.
# Two writers race here every day: marketing-publish.yml sweeps every 30 min and
# the ~22:30Z nightly commits the same paths. Without union merge the sweep's
# retry-rebase loop hit "CONFLICT (content): data/marketing/outbox/activity.jsonl"
# on all 5 attempts and gave up ("could not push ledger after 5 attempts"),
# stranding posted/approved transitions off main — which re-arms an already-sent
# item on the next sweep. Union is the only correct resolution for these files.
data/marketing/outbox/activity.jsonl merge=union
data/marketing/outbox/status_ledger.jsonl merge=union
data/marketing/outbox/items.jsonl merge=union
data/marketing/outbox/media_urls.jsonl merge=union
data/marketing/publications.jsonl merge=union
data/marketing/post_metrics.jsonl merge=union

# Hot Tape intraday state (masterplan §6). The radar commits both every 5 minutes
# from 13:25Z, straight into the same push-race the publish sweeps run in.
# The snapshot ring is the intraday history the "$X added in 3 hours" claims are
# built from — a dropped row is a hole in a claim we may make.
data/marketing/hot_tape_ring.jsonl merge=union
# The fired ledger IS the cooldown/dedupe memory — a dropped row re-arms an event
# that already posted, which is the one failure this lane must never have.
data/marketing/hot_tape_fired.jsonl merge=union

# Actions press wire state (masterplan §10 E7, scripts/marketing_press_wire.py).
# Committed BECAUSE the lane runs in Actions: every run is a fresh checkout, so
# state left in the daemon's gitignored data/marketing/press/ evaporates — and an
# evaporating spend counter means the $75/mo twitterapi.io cap is never enforced,
# because every run starts the month at $0.00.
#
# spend.jsonl carries DELTAS, not running totals, exactly so union is the correct
# resolution: two runs that appended in the same push race keep both rows and the
# month sums right. A running total would have one row win and the loser's spend
# would vanish — under-counting money is the one direction a cap must not fail in.
data/marketing/press_wire/spend.jsonl merge=union
# The seen ring is the dedupe memory for BOTH key spaces (the press lane's
# mirror-collapsed emission keys and breaking_feed's feed ids). A dropped row
# re-posts a story that already went out; it also lets the same headline register
# 288 arrivals a day into the burst detector. Union is the only safe resolution.
data/marketing/press_wire/seen_ring.jsonl merge=union
# NOTE: cursors.json is deliberately NOT union-merged — it is a whole-file JSON
# document with a single writer (this workflow's own concurrency group), and
# last-wins is correct there. Losing a cursors write costs one re-poll, which the
# seen ring above makes harmless.

# X competitive-intelligence corpus (masterplan §10 E3,
# engine/marketing/x_intel.py). Strictly APPEND-ONLY on disk and folded
# newest-per-tweet-id on read (x_intel.load_corpus), so a duplicated line is
# inert and a DROPPED line loses a post — and with it a week's engagement
# reading for that post — permanently. The weekly marketing-x-intel.yml run
# races the publish sweeps and the nightly on the same push, so union is the
# only correct resolution.
data/marketing/x_intel/corpus.jsonl merge=union
# NOTE: state.json, report.json, WEEKLY_REPORT.md and exemplar_store.json are
# deliberately NOT union-merged. They are whole-file documents with a single
# writer (this lane's own concurrency group) where last-wins is correct; a
# union-merged JSON document is a syntax error, not a merge.

# Eight-hex CSS files are byte-exact post-render artifacts. Their source Jinja
# intentionally retains indentation-only lines, so whitespace checks belong to
# the templates rather than to these content-addressed projections.
site/assets/css/????????.css whitespace=-trailing-space,-blank-at-eof

# CPI truth keeps exact content-addressed BLS HTML evidence. Upstream formatting
# is part of the pinned byte receipt, so source-style whitespace checks do not
# apply to these immutable archive objects.
data/release_forecast/cpi_truth/official_table1_archive/documents/sha256/*.html whitespace=-trailing-space,-space-before-tab

# The tier-gated page shells are WHOLE-FILE render output, and a 3-way TEXT
# merge of two different renders is a PAYWALL LEAK, not a merge. Every render
# lane pushes through `git pull --rebase --autostash -X theirs origin main`
# (closing-bell.yml, scripts/ci/push_retry.sh). `-X theirs` only decides
# CONFLICTING hunks — non-conflicting hunks from BOTH generations survive, so a
# rebase can splice one render's card grid onto another render's board data.
# Measured 2026-08-18 (main red on tests/test_us_board_gate.py): 33f7bdde0c3a
# rebased the closing-bell render onto a concurrent render-public push and
# produced a site/us_stocks.html whose grid carried FOUR cards against its own
# `preview: 3` — the 4th, ONTO, being row 0 of the LOCKED payload, published
# with full card detail plus a stale #us-stocktable-data block carrying the same
# locked row's paid record. Neither generation ever rendered that file; git
# assembled it. `-merge` makes these binary to the merge driver, so a rebase
# takes ONE render wholesale (under -X theirs, the replayed local one) instead
# of blending two. Losing the older generation is CORRECT here: the loser is a
# superseded render, and the next lane re-bakes the page regardless.
site/us_stocks.html               -merge
site/etfs.html                    -merge
site/special_situations.html      -merge
site/china_special_situations.html -merge
